  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "Bourse — a markets terminal for corporate compliance",
    "overview": "Bourse is a **markets terminal for corporate compliance.** Deep slate surface, a single\namber signal, monospaced tabular figures, ruled grids, a live statutory-deadline ticker.\nIt borrows the visual language of the exchanges the firm works with daily (NSE/BSE/NSDL/\nCDSL/NCLT) because the firm's edge is capital markets — IPO, SEBI, FEMA, corporate actions.\n\nThe aesthetic is deliberately distinctive-yet-serious. Its one risk is reading as a tech\nstartup or a crypto dashboard; the guardrail is **institutional restraint** — formal copy,\nstatutory precision, disciplined color, calm-but-crisp motion. Bloomberg terminal energy,\nlaw-firm gravitas. Mono is used because there is *real data* (deadlines, filing counts,\nregulation numbers), never as decoration.\n\nPersonality: **precise · disciplined · institutional.** The visitor should feel the firm\nwill not let them get it wrong. Adult professional readers need comfortable text, clear\nheadings and unmistakable actions. Readability takes precedence over extreme minimalism.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Institutional slate and amber palette",
      "Fraunces headings and wordmark, Geist prose, mono data",
      "Editorial capital-markets photography and a four-stage process",
      "Prominent vision/mission feature on About",
      "Coherent illustrative service photography with a distinct advisory asset"
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Amber Scalpel Rule",
        "body": "If more than a small percentage of a viewport is amber, cut it back. Live figures, the active deadline, the primary CTA, focus rings — that's the whole budget.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Visible Control Rule",
        "body": "Decorative dividers use `hairline`; actionable outlined controls and fields use `control-line`. Keep their boundaries visibly distinct from the surrounding surface. Use `on-signal` on `signal-solid` fills. Ordinary body text uses `ink` or `ink-dim`; `steel` remains secondary and `signal` remains an accent. ",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Reading First Rule",
        "body": "Keep prose comfortably readable and actions explicit before pursuing sparse styling. Increase wrapping or stack content on narrow screens; never shrink long names, legal copy or form text to fit a dense desktop arrangement.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Mono Names Data Rule",
        "body": "Mono labels identify actual figures, statutes, codes, deadlines or keyboard hints. They do not introduce prose headings as decorative eyebrows. Form labels, ordinary links, search text and explanatory copy use Geist. ",
        "section": "typography"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Keep amber to a small percentage of any viewport — live, active, figure, one CTA.",
      "Make every figure real and tabular; align numbers in columns with `tnum`.",
      "Use mono for *data* (labels, deadlines, codes, figures) and Geist for language.",
      "Build depth from tonal layers + hairlines; save shadow for overlays and glow for the one live element.",
      "Keep copy formal and statute-exact — the aesthetic's institutional guardrail lives in the words.",
      "Vary density while preserving 18px prose, clear 16px legal labels and generous targets.",
      "Keep explicit filled/outlined actions, default link underlines and visible keyboard focus."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "Don't let it read as SaaS/crypto: no decorative gradients, gradient text, emoji, playful copy, or big boxed hero-metrics. Local photographic reading shades are the approved exception.",
      "Don't use mono as a decorative eyebrow above prose headings — mono names data or it goes.",
      "Don't set reading prose in `steel` or `signal`; use `ink` or `ink-dim`.",
      "Don't turn the 25 services into separate identical cards. Keep them within the six practice areas, with direct detail links and governing statutes; the approved hub has six category cards.",
      "Don't add a second accent color; the discipline of one signal is the design.",
      "Don't animate floaty/bouncy or gate content visibility on reveal classes.",
      "Don't use side-stripe borders, glassmorphism, or arbitrary z-index (use a semantic scale)."
    ]
  }
